# NOTE (Landing 4 soft-cascade, 2026-07-14): `spawn(p: peer) -> torus`
# extends under Landing 4 to `spawn(s: subject_instance) -> torus`
# per docs/specs/subject-bauchladen-visibility-and-eigenboard-loop.md
# R1 (D3 "the @bauchladen moves from @peer onto @subject"). The
# `possessor` field admits subject_instance directly; the peer-only
# form is preserved as an ALIAS for one release cycle per two-tick
# discipline. Full collapse forward-promised to Tick N+1. @bauchladen
# composition is subject-general per Landing 4; peer-scoped alias
# preserved for one release cycle per two-tick discipline.

in @prism
in @meta
in @glass
in @bauchladen
in @autopoietic
in @glue
in @peer
in @epistemologic/cybernetic/eigenform
in @epistemologic/cybernetic/second_order

source @arxiv/cybernetics/foerster-2003
source @arxiv/cybernetics/kauffman-2003
source @arxiv/cybernetics/kauffman-1987
source @arxiv/cs/blum-blum-2022
source @arxiv/math/coquand-cohen-huber-mortberg-2018
source @arxiv/cybernetics/mcculloch-1945

prism @torus {
  focus  torus
  project torus
  split  torus
  shift  torus
  settle torus
}

# === The winding carrier — π₁(T²) = ℤ × ℤ ===
#
# The refined depth type. Replaces `depth: nat` in @third's
# observation_depth carrier at the marker altitude. Word-length in the
# abelian group ℤ × ℤ under the meridian/longitude basis:
#
#   depth-1 observation  = (1, 0) or (0, 1)
#   depth-2 observation  = (2, 0), (1, 1), or (0, 2)
#   depth-3 observation  = (m, n) with |m| + |n| = 3
#
# Composition is componentwise addition: (m₁, n₁) · (m₂, n₂) =
# (m₁ + m₂, n₁ + n₂). Abelian by construction (in fact a group). The
# substrate gets free associativity from the Rota-Baxter identity on
# the toroidal path algebra (§4.5 of the reframe).
#
# Signed counts, not absolute — a negative meridian count is a
# traversal in the opposite direction (Foerster's motor↔sensory axis
# admits both directions of causality; the neurohormonal axis likewise).
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on the (meridian_count,
# longitude_count) pair.
type winding = {
  meridian_count:  int,
  longitude_count: int,
}

# === The torus carrier — the peer's observation surface ===
#
# The typed record naming what a peer HAS at spawn. Four fields:
#
# - `possessor`  the peer whose observation surface this torus is. The
#                HAS relation is encoded here: the torus KNOWS its
#                possessor. This is what makes the relation possession
#                rather than reduction — the torus has no identity
#                independent of the peer that possesses it.
# - `meridian`   the canonical world-axis traversal generator. In
#                Foerster's derivation, the motor↔sensory closure. For
#                an agent peer, the world-observation axis. A loop_class
#                pointer into the peer's operational structure.
# - `longitude`  the canonical operator-axis traversal generator. In
#                Foerster's derivation, the neural↔hormonal closure.
#                For an agent peer, the observation-of-operators axis
#                — the loop that runs along the second generator, the
#                one that traverses the peer's own operator space
#                rather than the world.
# - `origin`     the basepoint on the torus. The peer's home position;
#                where every winding-class computation begins and
#                where every autonomous winding returns.
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on the (possessor, meridian,
# longitude, origin) quadruple. Two tori with the same possessor but
# different origins are distinct — the origin encodes the peer's
# current home position, which the peer may relocate via kintsugi
# under the operational discipline of @autopoietic closure.
type torus = {
  possessor: peer,
  meridian:  ref,
  longitude: ref,
  origin:    ref,
}

# === spawn — the peer HAS a torus ===
#
# The load-bearing action. Every peer possesses a torus at spawn. The
# HAS relation preserves peer identity: `spawn(p)` returns the torus
# `p` possesses, not a torus that `p` becomes. The peer stays the
# peer; the torus is the observation surface the peer OPERATES ON.
#
# Not `@glue(peer, @reflection, depth=n)` (O1 stack framing; ladder
# Foerster refused). Not `@glue(peer, peer)` (self-glue reading held
# open per §6.5, not resolved this tick). Just: the peer has one,
# possessed at spawn.
#
# For a fresh peer (not previously spawned), the torus is initialized
# with meridian and longitude derived from the peer's kind (human ↦
# sensorimotor + neurohormonal; agent ↦ world-axis + operator-axis;
# substrate ↦ input-axis + composition-axis). The origin is the peer's
# home. For an already-spawned peer, `spawn` returns the peer's
# extant torus (idempotence per the possession relation — a peer HAS
# one torus, not a fresh one each call).
#
# Body discharges per-species at the realization boundary.
spawn(p: peer) -> torus { \ }

# === traverse — run the peer's canonical observation ===
#
# Runs the peer's canonical observation along a winding class. The
# core of Foerster's "regulates its own regulation" — the peer
# traverses its own operational structure at the specified winding,
# producing a new torus whose origin has advanced by the winding.
#
# On an autonomous peer (autopoietic closure holds), every nontrivial
# winding class returns the same peer at the same identity — the
# origin may advance but the possessor is invariant. Failure of
# autonomy is failure of `autonomy(t, w)` below.
#
# Body discharges per-species at the realization boundary.
traverse(t: torus, w: winding) -> torus { \ }

# === autonomy — Foerster's regulation of regulation ===
#
# The bilateral predicate. Verdict: did the traversal return the same
# possessor? Foerster p. 238: "autonomy becomes synonymous with
# regulation of regulation. This is precisely what the doubly closed,
# recursively computing torus does: It regulates its own regulation."
# Autonomy on the torus IS the invariance of the possessor under every
# element of π₁(T²).
#
# Discharges via @autopoietic.autopoietic_closure_holds on the
# possessor. Fails as `partial(altitude-discipline)` when the
# invariance holds at the torus altitude but not at the peer altitude
# (a peer may lose autonomy on one axis while retaining it on the
# other — the winding-class-parametric refinement of #99 with
# altitude discipline per Mara's jspace observation §4).
autonomy(t: torus, w: winding) -> verdict { \ }

# === index_zero — Poincaré-Hopf balance ===
#
# The topological bilateral. Verdict: do the critical-point indices of
# the torus's canonical observation flow sum to χ(T²) = 0?
#
# For a compact oriented smooth surface M with an isolated-zero vector
# field X: Σ ind_p(X) = χ(M). For the torus, χ = 0. Consequence: on
# the torus, the substrate's flow can circulate everywhere without
# landing on a singularity (unlike S² where any tangent vector field
# has zero total index 2). This is Foerster's cognitive homeostasis.
#
# Substrate-decl consequence: `@onto = index-zero critical points on
# T²`. For every observer-attractor (positive index), a matched
# observer-repeller (negative index). Bilateral by topology. Splinter/
# Narcissus duality (void-dual-geometry.md) lives on the torus; their
# index sum is zero BECAUSE the surface is genus-1.
#
# Undecidable in general (§7.1 gap in the reframe). Discharge requires
# a bounded approximation or discrete torus model. Cubical HoTT's HIT
# may provide the tractable substrate; forward-promised.
index_zero(t: torus) -> verdict { \ }

# === torus_witnessing — composed bilateral ===
#
# The load-bearing bilateral consumers cite in `requires` clauses.
# Discharges three sub-predicates over torus:
#
#   autonomy(t, w) for every canonical winding w in the basis
#   index_zero(t)
#   @bauchladen.bauchladen_witnessing(t.origin) — the origin IS a
#     crystal in the substrate's tray; the torus reads its position
#     via the tray.
#
# All must hold for the composed verdict to pass. Failure modes
# surface as transparency<torus> per @glass discipline. The predicate
# name follows the substrate's `X_witnessing` pattern established at
# @bauchladen, @autopoietic, @fate, @cybernetic/eigenform,
# @cybernetic/second_order.
torus_witnessing(t: torus) -> verdict { \ }

out @torus
out winding
out torus
out spawn
out traverse
out autonomy
out index_zero
out torus_witnessing
